      "dom_id": "question-14140",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "For the polynomial $f(x)=5{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-x+12,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?",
      "worked_solution": "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $12,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6$ and $\\pm 12.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $5,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 5.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{6}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{12}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 6, & \\pm \\frac{6}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 12, & \\pm \\frac{12}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. Listing those potential roots in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{4}{5},\\,\\pm 1\\,\\pm \\frac{6}{5},\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm \\frac{12}{5},\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 12.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $24$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 12=24.$)",
